  "query_preview": "/Through Tuesday/...\nIssued at 1245 PM MDT Tue Sep 1 2026\n\nGOES-19 satellite imagery shows some cumulus clouds mainly in our \nfar western high country. Radar shows light rain lingering in far \neastern parts of our area as the shortwave continues to depart our \narea. SW flow aloft is still in place today, but PWATs are much \ncloser to average than the last couple of days. Isolated to perhaps \nscattered high-based showers or storms are possible today across \nmost of the area. The far eastern plains locations would have the \nhighest risk for a severe storm producing gusty outflow winds (given \nDCAPE approaching 1000 J/kg) and small hail. Although the risk is\nrather low, it is non-zero. MLCAPE values should reach around \n1000 J/kg and bulk shear will reach around 30 kts, only for the \neastern plains. High temperatures today will be in the mid 80s for\nthe plains.\n\nThere will be another chance for an isolated high-based shower or \nstorm tomorrow, but these are currently expected to stay mainly \nconfined to the high country. PoPs generally drop to below 10% for \nThursday. Otherwise, temperatures on the plains will warm into the \nupper 80s to low 90s on Wednesday and Thursday.\n\nBy Friday and Saturday, high pressure shifts closer to us allowing \ntemperatures to creep up into the low to mid 90s for the plains. \nMonsoonal moisture starts to make its return to the state on Friday \ninto the weekend aided by moisture associated with what will likely \nbe Hurricane Marie in the eastern Pacific. PWATs look to return to \nwell above average. As of now, rain chances appear to peak on \nSunday and Monday for our area with the greatest chances for our \nhigh country. Questions remain about how widespread rainfall will \nbe east of the Continental Divide this weekend. Ensemble guidance \nkeeps temperatures for the plains in the upper 80s and low 90s \nthrough at least Tuesday of next week.",
